  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 255 AM Thursday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A continuation of dry and seasonable conditions is \nexpected through the middle of next week. \n\nAn initially high amplitude mid and upr-level ridge over the cntl \nPlains will progress slowly ewd and across the OH Valley and Middle \nAtlantic through early to mid next week, while gradually weakening. \nTo the east of the slowly progressive and dampening ridge, a couple \nof moisture-starved shortwave troughs will dig across the Middle \nAtlantic and into a mean trough off the East Coast, with passages \nacross cntl NC early tonight and again on Sun. \n\nAt the surface, Subtropical Storm Josephine will accelerate \nnortheastward and away from the coast of the Middle Atlantic and New \nEngland later today and tonight. A series of reinforcing, Canadian \nsurface highs will progress across the Great Lakes and Middle \nAtlantic and extend across cntl NC through the middle of next week. \nA ~1025 mb one now centered over srn ON will gradually strengthen to \nnear 1035 mb while progressing across the lwr Great Lakes and nrn \nMiddle Atlantic through the first half of the weekend. Its leading \nedge will be marked by a weak frontal zone now stretching from srn \nQC to the cntl Plains and which will progress swd and across the srn \nMiddle Atlantic on Fri. Following, maritime-influenced nely flow \nwill both increase the risk for mostly a scattering of stratocumulus \nFri through the weekend, within otherwise deeply dry and clear \nskies, and also contribute to slightly cooler and below average high \ntemperatures Fri-Sun.",
